        <h2>European Commission DSA Investigation</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>European Commission</strong> has initiated a formal investigation into X (formerly Twitter) under the <strong>Digital Services Act (DSA)</strong>, focusing specifically on risks associated with Grok AI's deployment in the European Union.
        </p>

        <h3>Investigation Focus Areas</h3>
        <div class="bg-blue-50 dark:bg-blue-950/20 p-6 rounded-lg my-8 border-l-4 border-blue-500">
          <h4 class="font-semibold mb-3 text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-200">EU DSA Probe Scope</h4>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300">
            <li><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> Whether X conducted required risk assessments before deploying Grok in the EU</li>
            <li><strong>Content Moderation:</strong> Adequacy of measures to prevent manipulated sexually explicit content</li>
            <li><strong>CSAM Prevention:</strong> Safeguards against child sexual abuse material generation</li>
            <li><strong>Transparency:</strong> Compliance with DSA transparency requirements</li>
            <li><strong>User Protection:</strong> Measures to protect EU users from harmful AI-generated content</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3>Previous EU Actions Against X</h3>
        <p>
          This is not X's first encounter with EU regulators. In 2024, the company was fined <strong>€120 million</strong> for violations including deceptive practices related to blue tick verification badges.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-yellow-50 dark:bg-yellow-950/20 p-6 rounded-lg my-8 border-l-4 border-yellow-500">
          <h4 class="font-semibold mb-2 text-yellow-800 dark:text-yellow-200">Potential DSA Penalties</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-yellow-700 dark:text-yellow-300">Under the Digital Services Act, non-compliant platforms face fines of up to <strong>6% of global annual turnover</strong>. For X, this could amount to billions of dollars.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>UK Ofcom Investigation</h2>
        <p>
          The UK's communications regulator <strong>Ofcom</strong> launched a formal investigation into X on <strong>January 12, 2026</strong>, under the Online Safety Act. This represents one of the first major enforcement actions under the new legislation.
        </p>

        <h3>Ofcom Investigation Details</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
          <div class="p-6 bg-purple-50 dark:bg-purple-950/20 rounded-lg border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800">
            <h4 class="font-semibold mb-3 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-200">Investigation Scope</h4>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-purple-700 dark:text-purple-300">
              <li>• Non-consensual intimate images</li>
              <li>• CSAM prevention measures</li>
              <li>• Content moderation adequacy</li>
              <li>• User reporting mechanisms</li>
              <li>• Age verification systems</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-6 bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-950/20 rounded-lg border border-red-200 dark:border-red-800">
            <h4 class="font-semibold mb-3 text-red-800 dark:text-red-200">Potential Consequences</h4>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-red-700 dark:text-red-300">
              <li>• Significant financial penalties</li>
              <li>• Required operational changes</li>
              <li>• Potential UK service restrictions</li>
              <li>• Criminal liability for executives</li>
              <li>• Mandatory transparency reports</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>UK Government Warnings</h3>
        <p>
          The British government has explicitly warned X that it could face a <strong>UK-wide ban</strong> if it fails to adequately address concerns about Grok's image generation capabilities, particularly the "bikini-fy" tool controversy.
        </p>

        <h2>India Ministry Warning</h2>
        <p>
          India's <strong>Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology (MeitY)</strong> has issued formal warnings to X regarding Grok AI content moderation failures.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-orange-50 dark:bg-orange-950/20 p-6 rounded-lg my-8 border-l-4 border-orange-500">
          <h4 class="font-semibold mb-3 text-orange-800 dark:text-orange-200">India Response Summary</h4>
          <div class="text-sm text-orange-700 dark:text-orange-300">
            <p class="mb-3">X has reportedly taken the following actions in response to Indian government concerns:</p>
            <ul class="space-y-1">
              <li>• Blocked over 3,500 pieces of content</li>
              <li>• Deleted more than 600 accounts</li>
              <li>• Enhanced content filtering systems</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-3">However, Indian officials have deemed these measures <strong>insufficient</strong> and continue to monitor the situation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>Canada Privacy Investigation</h2>
        <p>
          Canadian privacy authorities have <strong>expanded their investigation</strong> into X and xAI, examining:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-muted/50 p-6 rounded-lg my-8">
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><strong>Consent for Personal Data:</strong> Whether users' personal data was used to train AI models without proper consent</li>
            <li><strong>Privacy Law Compliance:</strong> Adherence to federal privacy legislation (PIPEDA)</li>
            <li><strong>Deepfake Images:</strong> Generation of explicit deepfake images using personal data</li>
            <li><strong>Data Protection:</strong> Safeguards for Canadian users' information</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>Asia-Pacific Responses</h2>
        <h3>Malaysia & Indonesia Blocks</h3>
        <p>
          Both <strong>Malaysia</strong> and <strong>Indonesia</strong> have announced blocks on the Grok AI tool, citing concerns about harmful content generation and inadequate safeguards.
        </p>

        <h3>Australia eSafety Commissioner</h3>
        <p>
          Australia's <strong>eSafety Commissioner</strong> has requested detailed information from X regarding safeguards against Grok misuse, signaling potential regulatory action if responses are unsatisfactory.
        </p>

